                    "comment": "t.filter_bank - split streams into frequency bands"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "text_block",
                    "id": 311,
                    "position_x": 1122,
                    "position_y": 45,
                    "width": 768,
                    "height": 1591,
                    "visibility": "show_all",
                    "draggable": true,
                    "presentation_state": "show_all",
                    "properties": {
                        "0": {
                            "name": "block",
                            "value": "Splits each incoming stream into frequency bands with a bank of IIR filters (Butterworth or Chebyshev), sample by sample - slow movement in one band, fast in another.\n\nUse it to ask WHICH timescale a movement is on, not just how much there is: a slow sway and a fast tremor become separate numbers instead of one. Feed it one sample per frame; every value in the input gets its own complete bank.\n\nTHE BANDS:\nlow and high bound the range; 'number of bands' is the number of band EDGES spread between them, so it makes one band FEWER: the default 8 makes 7 bands. With log scaling (the default) the edges are evenly spaced in octaves; with linear, evenly in Hz. The output lists the bands from the HIGHEST down. 'overlap fraction' widens each band past its edges by that fraction of a band's width. Send the message report_bands to print each band's low, centre and high frequency in the console.\n\nWhat each band passes depends on filter type:\n   bandpass   between its two edges (the default)\n   bandstop   everything but its two edges' span\n   lowpass    everything below its upper edge\n   highpass   everything above its lower edge\n\nSAMPLE FREQ MUST BE RIGHT:\nThe bands are in Hz only if 'sample freq' is the rate the samples really arrive at. The demo assumes the patch runs at 60 frames a second; at another rate every band lands in the wrong place, with no warning. high is held below half the sample frequency.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.filter_bank\nNo arguments.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.filter_bank\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nsignal:\nA 2-D tensor (or array or list), [rows, columns]: one frame of values. A single value must be shaped [1, 1]; a 1-D input prints an error in the console and sends nothing. Each arrival is one sample for every value.\n\nreset (button):\nOn the next frame, fills all the filters' memory with that frame's values, as if the signal had always been there. Use it to avoid the start-up surge when a stream begins far from zero.\n\nfilter type (menu): bandpass, lowpass, highpass, bandstop. See above.\n\nfilter design (menu): butter (flat, the default), cheby1 (steeper, with 1 dB of ripple in the passband), cheby2 (steeper, with ripple in the stopband, which is held at least 40 dB down - a hundredth of the size;\nfor cheby2 the band edges are where that rejection starts, so a band passes less of its own range than with butter).\n\norder (int, 1 to 8, default 1):\nHow steeply each band cuts off. Higher separates the bands more cleanly and takes longer to settle.\n\nlow / high (Hz, defaults 0.01 and 12):\nThe range the bands cover. low must be above 0.\n\nnumber of bands (int, default 8): the number of band edges - one more than the number of bands.\n\nband scaling (menu): log or linear.\n\noverlap fraction (float, default 0).\n\nsample freq (Hz, default 60).\n\nChanging any setting rebuilds the filters (their memory starts empty).\n\ndevice / dtype / requires_grad (options):\nWhere the filters run and their number type (float32).\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nfiltered:\n[rows, columns, bands] - every value, once per band, highest band first.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe input is three sines added together: 0.5 Hz, 1.7 Hz and 6 Hz. With low 0.3, high 12, 4 band edges (3 bands) and order 2 the bands are about 3.5 to 12 Hz, 1 to 3.5 Hz and 0.3 to 1 Hz, and each picks out one sine: measured, line 1 swings at 6 Hz, line 2 at 1.7 Hz, line 3 at 0.5 Hz, with a seventh to a fifth of the neighbouring sines leaking in. Change filter type to lowpass and each line becomes everything below its band's top: line 1 is then nearly the whole input. With highpass each line is everything above its band's bottom, so it is line 3 that carries nearly the whole input and line 1 only the 6 Hz sine. Change filter design to cheby2 and the separation is much cleaner - the other sines are held to about 1 percent - but the band edges then mark where that rejection begins, so at order 2 each band passes its own sine at only about 0.6 of its size.\n\nRELATED:\nspectrum and band_pass do this for plain numbers; t.rotation_band_diff applies the same banding to joint rotations; t.rfft analyses a whole block at once instead of sample by sample.",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        },
                        "1": {
